    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Metropolitan areas require attention to relationships between a central city, surrounding settlements, and shared infrastructure. The historical development of Tehran and Karaj illustrates why municipal boundaries and changing census geographies complicate comparisons of urban population.",
    "tags": ["Metropolitan areas", "Tehran", "Karaj", "Urbanization", "Census geography"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:48:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:48:40Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Metropolitan Areas in Iran: Tehran, Karaj, and Urban Boundaries | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the growth of the Tehran–Karaj urban region and understand why city, county, and metropolitan population totals require different boundaries."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "beyond-city-name",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Start with the boundary",
        "content": "A metropolitan population figure needs a geographic definition before it can be interpreted. For this guide, the metropolitan perspective means looking beyond a central municipality to the surrounding settlements and infrastructure associated with it; it does not imply that one official boundary applies to every Iranian city. Karaj’s historical development beside Tehran demonstrates the problem particularly well: urban growth, transport connections, and administrative changes do not necessarily follow the same geography. [s1][s2]\n\nThe first question to ask of a population total is therefore what it counts. A named city and a larger administrative district are different units. Combining them without checking their boundaries can count some residents twice or omit others. The reconstruction undertaken in Habibollah Zanjani’s study of Karaj shows that this is a practical problem in Iranian census analysis, rather than a merely technical qualification. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-network",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tehran within a wider urban system",
        "content": "Iran’s large cities have developed within a wider hierarchy of regional centers. Eckart Ehlers’s historical synthesis identifies Tehran’s exceptional metropolitan role while also discussing important provincial centers such as Isfahan, Mashhad, Tabriz, and Shiraz. Its classifications describe an earlier period and should not be read as a current ranking. [s3]\n\nThe account connects postwar urban expansion with industrial development, new residential districts, and changes to infrastructure. It also describes how industrial concentration strengthened the capital region while development extended to other cities. This historical perspective helps explain why a metropolitan landscape contains more than a historic center: factories, housing districts, transport corridors, and older settlements became parts of an expanding urban geography. It does not, however, provide a contemporary commuting-based delineation of those regions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "karaj-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Karaj: from route settlement to major urban center",
        "content": "Bernard Hourcade’s account traces Karaj from a settlement and summer resort on the route between Tehran and Qazvin to a major industrial city. Roads through the Alborz mountains and along the Tehran–Karaj corridor connected it with wider markets and destinations. Industrial establishments and housing development subsequently reshaped the settlement. [s1]\n\nThis history cautions against treating Karaj only as an undifferentiated extension of Tehran. Its factories, residential districts, and earlier settlement sites have their own development histories. Hourcade also records changes in municipal organization, including the separation and later reunion of nearby settlements. These changes demonstrate how an apparently simple comparison of city totals can conceal alterations to the area being counted. His account is historical evidence, not confirmation of today’s municipal limits. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparable-populations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Growth must be separated from boundary change",
        "content": "Zanjani explicitly warns that changes in Karaj’s administrative boundaries prevent straightforward comparison of its earlier and later census totals. His solution is to reconstruct earlier populations using a common later geography, within the limits of the available information. This distinguishes demographic change from the effect of transferring territory between administrative units. [s2]\n\nThe same discipline is necessary before publishing a metropolitan trend: identify the component areas, document boundary changes, and retain the original census reference periods. A city-proper series cannot silently become a metropolitan series halfway through a chart. Until comparable boundaries and observations are verified, a carefully qualified account of urban development is more informative than an apparently precise population ranking. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
